(1, 1, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Most of the oxygen carried in arterial blood is:', '["Carried as bicarbonate", "Bound to haemoglobin", "Bound to albumin", "Dissolved in plasma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CaO2 = (Hb × SaO2 × 1.34) + (PaO2 × 0.003 per mmHg). Almost all of it is the haemoglobin-bound part.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 2, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Professional nurses are responsible for making clinical decisions to', '["Apply clear textbook solutions to patients'' problems.", "Prove traditional methods of providing nursing care to patients.", "Formulate standardized care plans for groups of patients", "Take immediate action when a patient''s condition worsens."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Taking prompt clinical action as a patient''s condition changes reflects professional clinical decision-making.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 3, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'In the treatment of alcohol withdrawal there are three line management', '["Supportive, Medical procedure and Medication", "Supportive, Medication and Special procedure", "Supportive group, medical management", "Medication, Special therapy and Group therapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Alcohol withdrawal management follows supportive care, medical procedures, and medication together.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 4, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Preload is best defined as:', '["The wall tension the ventricle develops during ejection", "The intrinsic contractility of heart muscle", "The end-diastolic volume of the ventricle", "The aortic diastolic pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Preload is the ventricular end-diastolic volume (fibre stretch) before contraction.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 5, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'The patient has vomiting that is very similar to coffee grounds. Where can the location of the bleeding be?', '["Large Intestine (colon)", "Rectum", "Upper GI Tract", "Small Intestine"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Coffee-ground emesis reflects digested blood from an upper GI source.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 6, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Early postoperative complication of thyroidectomy includes:', '["Hemorrhage", "Keloid", "Hypothyroidism", "Tracheomalacia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Haemorrhage is a recognised early (not late) complication after thyroidectomy.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 7, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'BMI of 25 -- 29.9 kg/m2 indicates', '["morbid obesity", "obese 2", "Overweight", "obese 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A BMI of 25-29.9 is classified as overweight.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 8, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'In accordance with American Heart Association resuscitation 2022 guide-line, the current concept of cardio-pulmonary resuscitation is:', '["ABCD", "HHHABCD", "ABCDHHH", "CABD"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Current AHA guidance emphasises compressions-first (CAB) sequencing in CPR.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 9, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Postural drainage positions are based specifically on:', '["Patient preference alone", "Bronchopulmonary segmental anatomy", "Blood pressure readings", "Random rotation regardless of anatomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each of the 12 positions is designed to match a specific lung segment''s anatomical drainage pathway.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 10, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Which of the following is not immediate life-threatening injuries', '["Tension pneumothorax", "Open pneumothorax", "Airway obstruction", "Liver injury"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Liver injury is a potentially (not immediately) life-threatening injury, unlike airway obstruction or pneumothorax which are immediate threats.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 11, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Which of the following factors can cause hepatitis A', '["Sexual contact with an infected person.", "Eating contaminated food", "Blood transfusions with infected blood.", "Contact with infected blood."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hepatitis A spreads via the faecal-oral route, commonly through contaminated food.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 12, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'In critical care, what is the primary role of a diuretic medication?', '["C) Promoting urine output", "D) Reducing oxygen levels", "B) Increasing heart rate", "A) Elevating blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Diuretics work primarily by promoting urine output.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 13, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'If pneumothorax is greater than ------%, then a chest tube is considered as an option of management.', '["15%", "18%", "20%", "22%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pneumothorax greater than about 20% typically warrants chest tube insertion.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 14, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'What is the recommended maximum suction pressure for children?', '["140 mmHg", "120 mmHg", "130 mmHg", "150 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Recommended suction pressures: adults 100–150 mmHg, children 80–120 mmHg, infants 60–100 mmHg.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 15, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Which of the following is the anticoagulant of choice for blood gas studies?', '["B. Lithium heparin", "D. Ammonium oxalate 5.0%", "A. Sodium citrate 3.2%", "C. Sodium citrate 3.8%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lithium heparin is the preferred anticoagulant for blood gas sampling.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 16, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'The adverse effect of mechanical ventilator is;', '["All are correct", "Renal effects", "Cardiovascular effects", "Pulmonary effects"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mechanical ventilation can affect the cardiovascular, pulmonary, and renal systems.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 17, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'The process of converting a message into words, pictures or gestures is known as:', '["Medium", "Decoding", "Feedback", "Encoding"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Encoding turns an idea into symbols (words, pictures, gestures) to send. Decoding is how the receiver interprets them.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 18, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'The controlling function finds out how far __________ deviates from standards.', '["Cost", "Improvement", "Corrective actions", "Actual performance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Controlling compares actual performance with the set standards to find deviations, then corrects them.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 19, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'A test that shows positive when the true status is negative gives a:', '["True positive", "False positive", "True negative", "False negative"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A false positive is a positive test result when the true status is negative.', 'Test Run'),
(1, 20, 'Test Run — Critical Care Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Critical Care Nursing', 'Research conducted to find a solution to an immediate problem is:', '["Analytical research", "Fundamental research", "Action research", "A survey"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Action research is done to find a solution to an immediate, practical problem in a setting.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 1, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A postoperative patient is found to have signs of infection at the surgical site. What is the most appropriate action by the nurse?', '["Administer prescribed antibiotics", "Apply ice to reduce swelling", "Perform frequent dressing changes", "Keep the wound dry and uncovered"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Prescribed antibiotics treat the underlying infection at the surgical site.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 2, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient is at risk for developing post-operative deep vein thrombosis (DVT). What nursing interventions can help prevent DVT in the post-operative period?', '["Monitor for signs and symptoms of DVT, such as swelling or pain in one leg", "Encourage early mobilization and activity", "Administer anticoagulant medications as prescribed", "Apply pneumatic compression devices to promote circulation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Prescribed anticoagulant medication reduces clot formation risk in patients at risk for postoperative DVT.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 3, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient in the ICU is at risk for developing delirium. What nursing interventions can help prevent delirium?', '["Keeping the environment quiet and calm", "Administering sedatives regularly", "Restraining the patient to prevent wandering", "Limiting visitation from family members"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A calm, quiet environment with orientation cues helps prevent and manage ICU delirium.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 4, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient is at risk for developing a pressure ulcer following surgery. What nursing interventions can help prevent pressure ulcers in the post-operative period?', '["Encourage the patient to lie on the affected side", "Turn and reposition the patient regularly", "Apply direct pressure to bony prominences", "Use tight dressings to prevent skin breakdown"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Regular turning and repositioning relieves pressure over bony prominences, reducing pressure ulcer risk.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 5, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient in the ICU develops signs of hypovolemic shock postoperatively. What interventions should the nurse prioritize?', '["Administering vasopressors", "Monitoring vital signs closely", "Elevating the legs", "Increasing IV fluid rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Increasing IV fluid administration restores circulating volume in hypovolaemic shock.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 6, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'Which anaesthetic agent can raise intracranial pressure?', '["Sevoflurane", "Thiopentone sodium", "Propofol", "Lignocaine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Volatile agents such as sevoflurane are cerebral vasodilators and can raise intracranial pressure. Propofol, thiopentone and lignocaine lower it.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 7, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'In 70 kg patient one unit of platelet concentrate should increase platelet count:', '["More than 20000 / mm3", "5000 - 10000 / mm3", "2000 - 5000 / mm3", "10000 - 20000 / mm3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One unit of platelet concentrate is generally expected to raise the platelet count by roughly 5,000-10,000/mm3 in an average adult.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 8, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A postoperative patient is experiencing paralytic ileus. What nursing intervention is appropriate for managing this complication?', '["Providing high-fiber diet", "Encouraging ambulation as tolerated", "Maintaining NPO status", "Administering antiemetic medications"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Keeping the patient NPO reduces gastric contents and vomiting risk while bowel motility is absent during paralytic ileus.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 9, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient in the ICU presents with symptoms of paralytic ileus. What nursing action should be prioritized?', '["Monitoring bowel sounds", "Administering antiemetic medication", "Maintaining nothing-by-mouth status", "Encouraging early ambulation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Early ambulation stimulates peristalsis and helps resolve paralytic ileus.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 10, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A renal transplant recipient on tacrolimus is at particular risk of:', '["Hypercoagulability from a strong immune system", "Increased antibody production and allergy", "Weight loss and persistent hypotension", "Infection, nephrotoxicity and hyperglycaemia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug levels are monitored; interactions with antifungals and antibiotics are common.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 11, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A nurse is caring for a sedated patient. The nurse should be aware that the patient is at risk for which of the following?', '["A decrease in length of stay in the ICU", "Decreased risk of aspiration", "Pressure ulcers and skin breakdown", "An increased level of consciousness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A sedated patient has a decreased level of consciousness and may be immobile for prolonged periods. This makes them highly susceptible to pressure ulcers and skin breakdown. The nurse must perform frequent repositioning and skin assessments.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 12, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient develops signs of nutritional deficiencies postoperatively. What intervention should the nurse prioritize?', '["Ordering a protein-rich diet", "Initiating total parenteral nutrition (TPN)", "Consulting a dietitian", "Administering vitamin supplements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A dietitian consultation supports individualised nutritional assessment and repletion planning for a patient with postoperative nutritional deficiency.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 13, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient is at risk for developing deep vein thrombosis (DVT) following surgery. What nursing interventions can help prevent DVT in the post-operative period?', '["Keep the patient well-hydrated to prevent blood clots", "Apply compression stockings as ordered", "Encourage the patient to remain sedentary and avoid moving around", "Elevate the patient''s legs above heart level at all times"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Graduated compression stockings promote venous return and reduce DVT risk in postoperative patients.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 14, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A patient is at risk for developing post-operative delirium. What nursing interventions can help prevent or manage post-operative delirium?', '["Ensure a quiet and well-lit environment for the patient", "Encourage the patient to stay awake for longer periods of time", "Limit the patient''s fluid intake to decrease confusion", "Administer sedatives to keep the patient calm"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A calm, appropriately lit, and well-oriented environment helps prevent or manage postoperative delirium.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 15, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A trauma patient has HR 130, falling systolic BP, RR 35 and confusion. The estimated blood loss class is:', '["Class III (30–40% of blood volume)", "Class I (up to 15% of blood volume)", "No loss; this is anxiety", "Class II (15–30% with normal BP)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Class III usually needs blood transfusion.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 16, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'What is the generally recommended target cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) in the management of severe traumatic brain injury?', '["90-100 mmHg", "60-70 mmHg", "Greater than 100 mmHg", "40-50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Current guidelines recommend maintaining CPP in the range of 60-70 mmHg to ensure adequate cerebral perfusion while avoiding the complications of excessive fluid and vasopressor use.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 17, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'Tranexamic acid in major trauma is most effective when given:', '["Only after surgery is completed", "Only once the INR exceeds 3", "After 8 hours of injury", "Within 3 hours of injury"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The CRASH-2 trial showed benefit within 3 hours and possible harm after.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 18, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'The most appropriate statements regarding the treatment of necrotizing soft tissue infections include:', '["The overall evidence for the mortality benefit of hyperbaric oxygen therapy is very effective, particularly for Clostridium spp.", "Reconstructive surgery should be considered as emergency", "When methicillin resistant S. aureus (MRSA) is suspected, linezolid is preferred to vancomycin as it inhibits exotoxin production.", "Intravenous immunoglobulin cannot bind to staphylococcal- and streptococcal-derived exotoxin."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reconstructive/debridement surgery is treated as a surgical emergency in necrotizing soft tissue infection.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 19, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'A key nursing intervention to prevent line sepsis is:', '["Changing dressings only when they fall off", "Leaving three-way taps open and uncapped", "Reusing syringes on the same patient''s line", "Scrubbing the hub before every access"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Use 2% chlorhexidine in alcohol for 15 seconds.', 'Test Run'),
(2, 20, 'Test Run — Perioperative Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Perioperative Nursing', 'An escape of blood from a blood vessel is called?', '["Infection", "Inflammation", "Hemorrhage", "Shock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Blood escaping from a vessel is, by definition, haemorrhage.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 1, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Under anaesthesia, the anaesthetist controls the head during transfer mainly to:', '["Protect the airway and cervical spine", "Check the patient''s identity band against the list again", "Count the transfer staff aloud", "Keep the patient''s eyes closed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The team moves on the anaesthetist''s command.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 2, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Theatre traffic is best controlled by:', '["Encouraging staff to change rooms frequently", "Limiting the number of people and movement during surgery", "Keeping the theatre doors propped wide open", "Allowing students to walk freely in and out so they can see each step"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fewer people means fewer airborne bacteria.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 3, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Hand hygiene before putting on sterile gloves for minor procedures can be done with:', '["An alcohol-based surgical hand rub, if the hands are visibly clean", "Nothing at all, as the gloves themselves are sterile", "A dry paper towel only, wiping each finger and the palm in turn for a minute", "Plain tap water only, as gloves will cover the hands"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Wash with soap and water first if the hands are soiled.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 4, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'According to the Spaulding classification, a flexible gastroscope (which contacts mucous membranes) requires, at minimum:', '["High-level disinfection", "Low-level disinfection", "Cleaning with detergent only", "Sterilisation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Critical items (enter sterile tissue or blood) must be sterile; semi-critical items (contact mucous membranes or non-intact skin) need at least high-level disinfection; non-critical items (intact skin) need low-level disinfection.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 5, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'A ''neutral zone'' for sharps in theatre means:', '["Needles are recapped before being passed back", "Sharps are left anywhere on the drapes to save time", "The surgeon keeps all the needles in one hand so they are never dropped", "Sharps are placed in a set area, not passed hand to hand"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This hands-free technique reduces needlestick injuries.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 6, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'A patient s/p abdominal surgery has a BP of 85/50 mmHg and HR 120 bpm. The nurse suspects:', '["Atelectasis", "Pain", "Infection", "Hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Tachycardia and hypotension indicate hypovolemia (e.g., bleeding).', 'Test Run'),
(3, 7, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Before a laser is used in theatre, the nurse must ensure:', '["The alcohol skin preparation is still wet when the laser is started", "Reflective metal instruments are used nearby", "The windows are uncovered for natural light", "Correct eyewear for that laser and warning signs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Damp swabs around the site and non-reflective instruments reduce fire and reflection risk.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 8, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'At the end of an operation, before the patient leaves theatre, the Sign Out confirms:', '["The patient''s fasting time and last food or drink", "Procedure, counts, specimens and recovery concerns", "Patient identity and consent, checked before induction", "The allergy status, confirmed before the anaesthetic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sign Out is led by a team member, often the nurse.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 9, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'In laparoscopic surgery, the abdomen is usually insufflated with:', '["Oxygen, so the bowel stays pink", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrous oxide", "Room air"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CO₂ is non-flammable and quickly absorbed.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 10, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Which observation by the circulating nurse is a break in sterile technique?', '["Two scrubbed people pass each other back to back", "Scrubbed staff keep their hands above waist level", "A scrubbed person turns their back to the field", "The scrub nurse faces the sterile field at all times"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The back of the gown is not sterile.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 11, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Which item is a correct rule for theatre attire?', '["Jewellery is removed and all hair is covered", "Scrubs worn outside are fine without change", "Long nails are allowed if gloves are worn", "Masks may hang around the neck between cases"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rings and long or artificial nails harbour bacteria and tear gloves.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 12, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Shoulder-tip pain after laparoscopy is due to:', '["Residual CO₂ irritating the diaphragm", "A myocardial infarction during surgery", "Brachial plexus stretch from the arm boards", "A diathermy burn on the shoulder skin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Referred to C3–C5; it settles in 1–3 days.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 13, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Which of these is Not Opioid Analgesic Drug', '["Pethidine", "Ketorolac", "Fentanyl,", "Morphine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ketorolac is an NSAID, not an opioid, unlike morphine, pethidine, and fentanyl.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 14, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Ipratropium bromide is used in the management of:', '["Acute peptic ulcer disease", "Acute bronchopneumonia", "Acute asthmatic attack", "Acute gastroenteritis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ipratropium bromide is an inhaled anticholinergic bronchodilator, added to salbutamol in acute severe asthma and used in COPD.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 15, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Propofol infusion syndrome is most associated with:', '["A single induction dose given to a healthy adult", "Low doses used for short day-case procedures", "High doses for more than 48 hours, causing acidosis and rhabdomyolysis", "Diluting propofol with 0.9% normal saline"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Signs: metabolic acidosis, raised CK, hyperkalaemia, arrhythmias and heart failure. Monitor triglycerides.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 16, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Ventricular compliance is defined as:', '["Change in pressure ÷ change in volume", "Stroke volume ÷ end-diastolic volume", "Cardiac output ÷ body surface area", "Change in volume ÷ change in pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Compliance = change in volume ÷ change in pressure. A stiff (non-compliant) ventricle has higher filling pressure at the same volume.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 17, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'A farmer presents with goitre and reports eating cassava regularly. Which mechanism explains his condition?', '["Cassava reduces TRH secretion", "Cassava inhibits iodine trapping", "Cassava increases TSH secretion", "Cassava enhances thyroglobulin coupling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cassava contains cyanogenic compounds that interfere with the thyroid''s ability to trap iodine.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 18, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'A patient arrives in the emergency department with a suspected cervical spinal cord injury. Which of the following is an assessment priority?', '["Neurological assessment", "Cardiac assessment", "Respiratory assessment", "Abdominal assessment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In suspected cervical spine injury, protecting the airway/breathing (with spinal precautions) is the priority.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 19, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'Which of the following statements is false regarding pneumothorax?', '["It may be a complication of lung biopsy", "It appears as area of hypertransradiancy devoid of lung markings", "Mediastinum is shifted to the opposite side", "Mediastinum is shifted to the side of lesion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In pneumothorax, the mediastinum shifts AWAY from (not toward) the affected side — that statement is false.', 'Test Run'),
(3, 20, 'Test Run — Anaesthesia Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Anaesthesia Nursing', 'A respiratory condition characterized by accumulation of air in the pleural cavity, (Space between the visceral and parietal pleura of the lung) is called;', '["Pulmonary accumulation", "Pyeothorax", "Pneumothorax", "Pulmonary oedema"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Air accumulation in the pleural space is pneumothorax.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 1, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A nurse is interpreting ABG results for a patient and notes a normal PaO₂ and a high PaCO₂. This is an example of:', '["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Normal findings", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A normal PaO₂ and a high PaCO₂ indicate that the patient is able to oxygenate but is not able to ventilate effectively. The high PaCO₂ causes respiratory acidosis. If the pH is low and the HCO₃⁻ is normal, it is uncompensated.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 2, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'Which vascular access is generally preferred for long-term hemodialysis due to lower infection and thrombosis rates compared with other options?', '["Tunneled central venous catheter", "Peripheral IV line", "Arteriovenous fistula", "Temporary femoral catheter"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An arteriovenous fistula, created by surgically connecting an artery and vein, has the lowest rates of infection and thrombosis and the best long-term patency of all access types.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 3, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A patient with hypernatremia (Na⁺ 160 mEq/L) is lethargic. Which fluid should the nurse administer?', '["0.9% saline", "D5W", "3% saline", "Lactated Ringer’s"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypernatremia requires free water (e.g., D5W) to correct hypertonicity. Saline solutions worsen hypernatremia.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 4, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A passive leg raise predicts fluid responsiveness when it raises stroke volume or cardiac output by more than:', '["10%", "1%", "50%", "100%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Passive leg raising returns about 300 mL of blood from the legs, and the effect is reversible.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 5, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'The target urine output in an adult burns patient during fluid resuscitation is:', '["2–3ml/kg/hr", "0.1–0.2ml/kg/hr", "0.5–1.0ml/kg/hr", "5ml/kg/hr"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Urine output is the most practical bedside marker of adequate fluid resuscitation, and 0.5–1.0ml/kg/hr is the target range in adults.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 6, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A FAST ultrasound scan in trauma looks for free fluid in the pericardium, right and left upper quadrants and:', '["Pelvis", "Spinal canal", "Thyroid", "Brain"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A positive FAST in an unstable patient usually means theatre.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 7, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'In a patient with respiratory failure, the nurse correctly interprets a high PaCO₂ value and a low pH from an ABG. This indicates:', '["Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A high PaCO₂ indicates hypoventilation, which causes a buildup of carbonic acid and results in a low pH, a classic sign of respiratory acidosis. This is a ventilation problem, not a metabolic one.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 8, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'The minimum amount of pleural fluid usually needed to be visible on a standard erect chest X-ray is:', '["1000–1500ml", "10–50ml", "500–750ml", "150–300ml"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Smaller amounts of fluid can hide without being visible on standard films.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 9, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'Which class of medication is well recognised as nephrotoxic and should be used cautiously in renal impairment?', '["Beta-blockers", "Antihistamines", "Aminoglycoside antibiotics", "Proton pump inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Aminoglycosides (e.g. gentamicin) are well-established nephrotoxins, along with NSAIDs and iodinated contrast media, requiring cautious dosing and monitoring in patients with renal impairment.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 10, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'Multiple organ failure (MOF) in an orthopaedic trauma patient reflects:', '["A benign, self-limiting condition", "An issue confined to the injured limb only", "Kidneys, liver, lungs, and heart failing together", "A single organ system affected in isolation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'MOF is a systemic collapse of multiple organ systems simultaneously, often triggered by severe trauma or its complications, and requires intensive multi-system support.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 11, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A patient with chronic pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure needs fluid management. The ICU nurse should expect:', '["Large crystalloid boluses to raise preload and push blood through the lungs", "Fluids guided only by hourly urine output, ignoring CVP and the echo", "Small, careful fluid boluses, as the RV tolerates under- and over-filling poorly", "Strict fluid restriction regardless of the blood pressure or the CVP"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An over-distended RV shifts the septum and reduces LV filling; too little preload drops output. Small, monitored boluses are safest.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 12, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A patient with heart failure receiving digoxin has a potassium level of 3.0mmol/L. The nurse should recognise this combination as significant because it:', '["Only affects blood pressure, not cardiac rhythm", "Reduces the risk of digoxin toxicity", "Has no bearing on digoxin''s effect", "Increases the risk of digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hypokalaemia increases myocardial sensitivity to digoxin, raising the risk of toxicity and arrhythmia even at otherwise therapeutic digoxin levels.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 13, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A patient with metabolic acidosis has K⁺ 5.8 mEq/L. Which intervention addresses BOTH imbalances?', '["Start kayexalate orally", "Administer sodium bicarbonate IV", "Infuse regular insulin IV", "Give calcium gluconate IV"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sodium bicarbonate corrects acidosis and shifts K⁺ intracellularly. Insulin also shifts K⁺ but does not correct acidosis.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 14, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'The KDIGO criteria for staging acute kidney injury are based primarily on changes in:', '["Serum sodium only", "Serum creatinine and urine output", "Blood pressure and heart rate", "Haemoglobin level"]'::jsonb, 1, 'KDIGO AKI staging uses the magnitude of serum creatinine rise and the degree/duration of reduced urine output to classify AKI severity.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 15, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'What is the antidote for insulin intoxication', '["Calcium lactate", "Manganese oxide", "Sodium", "Potassium"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Potassium/glucose (dextrose-insulin therapy issues) - Potassium replacement is a key part of managing insulin-induced hypoglycaemia/toxicity complications.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 16, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'A 53-year-old patient who is receiving ibuprofen 400 mg twice a day, for chronic, low back pain develops lower-extremity oedema. The pain management nurse suspects that the oedema is caused by:', '["A decrease in renal function", "An increase in plasma proteins.", "A low creatinine level.", "An increase in glomerular filtration rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'NSAIDs can reduce renal blood flow/function, causing fluid retention and oedema.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 17, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'The major risk of giving potassium chloride as an undiluted IV bolus is:', '["Arrhythmia or cardiac arrest", "Acute bronchospasm", "Profound hypothermia", "Severe hypoglycaemia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Concentrated potassium must always be diluted and infused by pump, usually no faster than 10 mmol/hour on a ward. It is a high-alert medicine stored separately.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 18, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'Foetal complications occurs in Eclampsia as a result of', '["Placenta pigmentation", "Placenta hypo-perfusion", "Placenta hyper perfusion", "Placental bleeding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reduced placental perfusion in eclampsia compromises foetal oxygen/nutrient supply, causing foetal complications.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 19, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'Hypotension in pregnancy is defined as a blood pressure below:', '["90/60 mmHg", "120/90 mmHg", "100/70 mmHg", "110/80 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hypotension in pregnancy is usually defined as BP below 90/60 mmHg.', 'Test Run'),
(4, 20, 'Test Run — Nephrology Nursing (Nov 2026)', 'Test Run · Nephrology Nursing', 'The definitive (gold-standard) method of confirming ectopic pregnancy is:', '["Ultrasound", "HSG", "Urine pregnancy test", "Laparoscopy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Transvaginal ultrasound with serum β-hCG is the main diagnostic tool. Laparoscopy is the definitive (gold-standard) confirmation.', 'Test Run');
